tools.description:
Get a single annotation (chart note) by its id. Pass annotationId (the UUID returned by add_annotation or list_annotations). Returns the date (Y-m-d), note text, description, category, linked task_id, author email, the property it belongs to (null for global/official notes), its URL scope (context, urls, urls_used, filters) and its keyword scope (keyword_context: all or list_of_keywords; keywords: the GSC queries a list_of_keywords note is restricted to). Set includeImpact=true to also compute the before/after GSC impact around the annotation date at 7/14/30-day windows (clicks, impressions, ctr, position — each with value_before, value_after, diff, change_pct), scope-aware (the note's urls, and only its keywords for a list_of_keywords note); costs 3 credits, and incomplete windows are flagged complete=false. For a pattern-scoped note (contains / starts-with / ends-with / regex) the top-level urls and urls_used stay empty — the URLs the numbers were computed over are only in the impact block (includeImpact=true): impact.urls_used is the 30-day window's top 100 URLs by clicks (urls_used_truncated when more matched) and each window carries windows[i].urls_used for its own date range; if resolving fails, urls_used is null with urls_used_unavailable and the numbers still stand. For a note with only literal URLs impact.urls_used lists those. Use list_annotations to find an id.

Cost: free.

Examples:
```json
// Fetch one annotation by the UUID from list_annotations.
{"annotationId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001"}

// Fetch an annotation with its 7/14/30-day before/after GSC impact inline.
{"annotationId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001","includeImpact":true}
```

List annotations (chart notes) for a property within a date range, newest first. Pass property (the UUID or sc-domain URL from list_properties). Date range: either range (last_7_days, last_28_days, last_3_months, last_year) or absolute from/to (Y-m-d); defaults to last_3_months when omitted. Optional: search (substring match on the note text), page and limit (pagination; limit capped at 200). Every row carries its URL scope (context, urls, urls_used, filters) and its keyword scope (keyword_context: all or list_of_keywords; keywords: the GSC queries a list_of_keywords note is restricted to). Set url to filter to the annotations attached to that page — single-URL notes, list-of-URLs notes containing it, and multi-condition notes whose conditions name the URL literally (exactly-matches / list-of-urls); sitewide notes are excluded (you get the URL's history, not the property's) and pattern conditions (contains / starts-with / ends-with / regex) never match — unless impactScope=per_url (below). Both sides are normalized (http/https, www, trailing slash, case, utm params), so a roughly-right URL still matches; urlMatch picks exact (default), prefix (plain string prefix — url .../blog also matches .../blog-old) or contains (substring; accepts a bare fragment like blog/post-1), and is ignored without url. The url_filter block echoes the normalized needle, the mode and total (the size of the whole matching set, not just this page). One URL per call — for several URLs, call in parallel. Set keyword to keep only the notes explicitly scoped to that GSC query (a list_of_keywords note containing it); all-keyword notes are excluded. keyword is compared exactly after trimming, collapsing whitespace and lowercasing — no substring or pattern matching; the keyword_filter block echoes the normalized keyword and total. url and keyword combine (a note must match both). Set includeImpact=true to attach each annotation's before/after GSC impact at 7/14/30-day windows (as in get_annotation); this caps the page to 10 rows (50 when url is set) and costs 3 credits per annotation. For a note attached to several URLs the impact block aggregates across all of the note's URLs (as in get_annotation), not only the queried one; a list_of_keywords note's impact counts only its keywords. Use a returned id with get_annotation for full detail. Set impactScope=per_url (with url) to look at one page. With urlMatch=exact it also lists the pattern-scoped notes (contains / starts-with / ends-with / regex, does-not-* included) whose conditions cover that page — evaluated exactly as the note's own impact query evaluates them (case-sensitive), against the page_url GSC stored for the page (scheme, www and trailing slash don't matter; a page with no GSC rows within the range ±30 days matches no pattern); prefix/contains needles never list pattern notes. Why: the note's own impact SQL decides, so a listed note always agrees with its numbers, and a prefix/contains needle is a URL fragment, not a page, so there is no page to test a pattern against. Under per_url a note is listed only when its whole condition covers the page — a multi-condition note that names the URL but whose AND conditions exclude it is left out. If that ClickHouse match fails (e.g. a stored regex ClickHouse rejects), only the fully literal notes are returned and url_filter.pattern_notes_unavailable=true. With includeImpact each note's impact is then computed for that page only: impact_scope=per_url and impact.urls_used lists the stored URL(s) the numbers ran over. For pattern-scoped notes (contains / starts-with / ends-with / regex) the row's top-level urls and urls_used stay empty — the resolved URL set is only in the impact block (includeImpact=true): impact.urls_used is the 30-day window's top 100 URLs by clicks (urls_used_truncated when more matched) and each window carries windows[i].urls_used for its own date range; if resolving fails, urls_used is null with urls_used_unavailable and the numbers still stand. impactScope=per_url without url is an error.

Cost: free.

Examples:
```json
// Recent annotations for a property over the last 3 months.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","range":"last_3_months","limit":25}

// One URL's annotation history with before/after GSC impact per note.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","url":"https://example.com/blog/post-1","includeImpact":true}

// Notes scoped to one GSC query, with before/after impact on that query.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","keyword":"running shoes","includeImpact":true}

// One URL's note history, each note's impact scoped to that URL only (impact_scope=per_url).
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","url":"https://example.com/blog/post-1","includeImpact":true,"impactScope":"per_url"}

// Also lists pattern notes covering the page (e.g. contains /articles/ after a robots.txt Disallow change), with their impact on that page alone.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","url":"https://example.com/articles/guide","includeImpact":true,"impactScope":"per_url"}
```

Create an annotation (chart note) on a property. Requires property (the property UUID or sc-domain URL from list_properties), date (Y-m-d) and note (the annotation text). Optional: description (longer body), category (an existing category name or UUID), taskId (a task UUID to link, from list_tasks). URL scope: context controls which URLs the note applies to — sitewide (default, whole property), single (one URL, pass exactly one in urls), or list_of_urls (several explicit URLs in urls; alias: several). urls is a list of full http(s) URLs, required for single/list_of_urls and must be empty for sitewide. Keyword scope: keywords is an optional list of GSC queries the note applies to (the web app's "List of Keywords"); omit it or pass [] for all keywords. The two scopes combine (AND) — a single-URL note with keywords covers only those queries on that page. Keywords match GSC queries exactly and case-sensitively (no patterns), so pass them as GSC reports them, e.g. from get_top_keywords; blanks and duplicates are dropped. The annotation is created as a custom note authored by the calling user. Requires the annotations:write scope.

Cost: free.

Examples:
```json
// create a sitewide note on a single date
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","date":"2026-05-01","note":"Published the new pricing page","description":"Launched the revamped pricing page with annual plans."}

// scope a note to one page and two GSC queries
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","date":"2026-05-01","note":"Rewrote the running-shoes buying guide","context":"single","urls":["https://example.com/guides/running-shoes"],"keywords":["running shoes","best running shoes"]}
```

Get Google Analytics 4 metrics per individual page (landing/URL) for a property, ranked so you can see which pages drive the most sessions, signups (conversions) or users. The page dimension is the GA4 page path. Each row returns the page plus sessions, users, conversions and engagement_rate (engaged sessions / sessions), each with the immediately preceding period (prev) and change_pct for period-over-period comparison. Accepts a date range as presets (last_7_days, last_28_days, last_3_months, last_year) or absolute dates (from/to in Y-m-d). Sort with sortBy = sessions (default), conversions or users; rows are capped by limit (default 25, max 100). `data_synced` is false when the property has no synced GA4 data yet (so an all-zero result is not mistaken for real zeros), and `data_start_date` is the earliest synced day — `partial_coverage.current` / `partial_coverage.previous` flag when a period starts before it, so the zeros and change_pct for that period are a coverage gap rather than a trend. Note: crossing pages with a traffic source/channel, and new_users / avg_session_duration per page, are not yet available. Pass the project ID (UUID) or URL from list_ga4_properties.

Cost: 5 credits per call.

Examples:
```json
// Top landing pages by GA4 sessions for the last 28 days vs the prior period.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","range":"last_28_days"}

// Top 10 pages by conversions (signups) over the last 3 months.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","range":"last_3_months","sortBy":"conversions","limit":10}
```

Create a task on a property. Requires title. Optional: description, assignee user id, priority (1-5, default 3), taskbox (column UUID or a status keyword: todo, in_progress, qa, done — case-insensitive; default TO-DO column), due_date (YYYY-MM-DD, the task Goal Date), parent (a ticket UUID, slug, or browser URL — creates the task as that ticket's subtask). To make the task an applyable native SEO change, also pass targetUrl (absolute page URL), field (one of: meta_title, meta_description, h1, canonical, robots_meta, og_title, og_description, image_alt) and proposedValue; optionally platform (shopify|webflow|wordpress), currentValue, rationale. Nothing is applied automatically — the change is only proposed and needs explicit approval.

Cost: 2 credits per call.

Examples:
```json
// create a task in the TO-DO column with a goal date
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","title":"Optimize the pricing page meta description","taskbox":"todo","dueDate":"2026-05-28"}

// create the task as a subtask of an existing ticket (referenced by slug).
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","title":"Draft the H1 variants","parent":"optimize-the-pricing-page"}

// create an actionable task carrying a proposed native SEO change (approval + apply happen later).
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","title":"Rewrite the pricing page meta title","targetUrl":"https://example.com/pricing","platform":"shopify","field":"meta_title","currentValue":"Pricing","proposedValue":"Pricing Plans & Costs (2026) — Example","rationale":"Low CTR at avg position 6; the current title is generic."}
```

List the SEO Reporting reports built for a property. Each item carries id, name, is_template, is_automate, created_at and owner. By default returns plain reports; pass templates=true to list report templates instead. Recurring/scheduled reports are excluded here — use list_report_schedules for those. Cursor-paginated: pass the returned next_cursor back as cursor to page. Use a report id with get_report or generate_report. Pass the property ID (UUID) or URL (sc-domain:...) from list_properties.

Cost: 2 credits per call.

Examples:
```json
// list the property's reports.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com"}

// list report templates instead of plain reports.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","templates":true}
```

Partially update an AI Tracker (Brand Radar) prompt. promptId is the prompt UUID (from list_prompts). Provide any combination of name (<= 1024 chars), model (comma-separated engines: chatgpt, openai, claude, anthropic, gemini, perplexity, ai_overview, ai_mode), country, responseLanguage, period (daily, week or month), topicId (move the prompt to an existing topic, from list_prompt_topics) or topicTitle (move it to the topic with that title, created if missing); omitted fields are left unchanged and no topic keeps the current one. Moving keeps the prompt history. The result must stay unique within the destination topic. Pass the property ID (UUID) or URL (sc-domain:...) from list_properties.

Cost: 2 credits per call.

Examples:
```json
// change only how often a prompt runs
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","promptId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001","period":"month"}

// move a prompt to another topic (family), keeping its history
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","promptId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001","topicId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002"}
```

List top pages of a property ranked by a chosen metric with period-over-period comparison. Accepts a date range as presets (last_7_days, last_28_days, last_3_months, last_year) or as absolute dates (from/to in Y-m-d format). Sort by clicks, impressions, position, or ctr. Returns all four metrics per page (clicks, impressions, ctr, position — each with current value, previous-period value, diff, and change_pct) and cursor pagination. Optional filters: country (ISO-3166 alpha-3, e.g. "usa"), device ("desktop"/"mobile"/"tablet"), position_min/position_max to keep only pages whose current-period average position falls in that range (applied regardless of sort, not combinable with country/device yet), tags (array of page tag names from list_tags — keeps only pages carrying any of those tags), and url_contains (case-insensitive text matched anywhere in the URL, same as the search box of the Pages report; a plain substring, so "hasta-5000" also matches "hasta-50000"). With url_contains the response adds "summary" with total_urls, clicks and impressions for every matching page in the period (not only the returned page), so one call sizes a URL segment; no match returns an empty list and zero totals. Position is returned on every row. The applied filters are echoed back under "filters". Pass the property ID (UUID) or URL (sc-domain:...) from list_properties.

Cost: 5 credits per call.

Examples:
```json
// Top 25 pages by clicks over the last 28 days.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","range":"last_28_days","sortBy":"clicks","limit":25}

// Total URLs, clicks and impressions of every /blog/ URL (summary), plus its top 10 pages.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","from":"2026-08-01","to":"2026-10-31","urlContains":"/blog/","limit":10}
```

Create a Crawler project for a property — the same setup the dashboard's Crawler form performs, and the same thing the other MCP tools call a Site Audit (list_site_audit_projects, get_site_audit_summary and trigger_site_audit_crawl all operate on these projects). WRITE action — requires the write scope. Creating a project crawls nothing and spends no crawler credits; it stores the configuration only. Start the first crawl with trigger_site_audit_crawl and pass the returned audit_id: the trigger tool otherwise targets the most-recently-finished project, and a brand-new one has never finished a crawl. Crawler credits are separate from MCP credits and are spent then, one per crawled page. Defaults applied, none of them configurable on any surface: desktop device, the SeoCrawlerBot user agent, and an automatic weekly re-crawl once the first crawl has run. Returns property, audit_id, name, mode, urls_count and status. Errors: invalid_crawler_project_name (empty or over 255 characters), unsupported_crawl_mode, urls_required, urls_not_allowed, urls_outside_property (malformed URL or one on another host), too_many_urls, property_host_unknown. Pass the property ID (UUID) or URL (sc-domain:...) from list_properties.

Cost: 1 credit per call.

Examples:
```json
// Create a project that discovers its own URLs, then start it with trigger_site_audit_crawl using the returned audit_id.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","name":"Full site crawl"}

// Crawl a fixed list of the property's own pages instead of the whole site.
{"property":"sc-domain:example.com","name":"Key landing pages","mode":"list","urls":["https://example.com/pricing","https://example.com/features"]}
```

Add a website as a new property from its domain alone — no Google Search Console needed — so you can use the AI Tracker on it right away: add prompts with create_prompt, then run them with trigger_prompt_run. The same thing "New Project → Add a website without Search Console" does in the app: the property is titled after the brand, gets one AI Tracker topic, and Search Console can be connected to the same property later in the app. WRITE action — requires the write scope. domain is the website (example.com; a pasted URL is reduced to its host); our own domains are refused. organization is optional: the organization's id or name, among the ones you belong to; omitted, it is the one you are working in (your active organization). Counts against that organization's plan: a trial or Free account has one website, a paid plan its normal project limit. Idempotent: a website the organization already has is returned with created=false. Returns property (the new property ID, to pass to the other tools), title, domain, created, organization {id, name} and url (its AI Tracker page). Errors: invalid_domain, organization_not_found, organization_ambiguous (use the id), project_limit_reached, trial_limit_reached (upgrade to add more), website_exists (the organization has it and you are not a member of it).

Cost: free.

Examples:
```json
// Add example.com to the organization you are working in, then create_prompt with the returned property.
{"domain":"example.com"}

// Add a client's website to a specific organization.
{"domain":"client-brand.com","organization":"SEOcrawl Team"}
```

Add a comment to a task. ticket_id accepts UUID, ticket slug, or a full browser task URL (property optional when ticket_id is a URL). visibility is "public" (default, visible to everyone on the board) or "internal" (team-only note: visible to admins and team members, never to clients; clients cannot post internal notes).

Cost: 1 credit per call.

Examples:
```json
// add a comment to a task by its UUID
{"ticketId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001","body":"Reviewed the draft, looks good to ship.","property":"sc-domain:example.com"}

// add a team-only internal note (never visible to clients)
{"ticketId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001","body":"QA: verified on staging, only the empty state is left.","visibility":"internal","property":"sc-domain:example.com"}
```
